12.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The position after 34.Kg5 is White Kg5 Qa5 Rd8 Nf4 g3 f3 vs Black Kg8 Rh2 Re8 Rb8 Ne5 g6 etc., Black to move.

Current round consensus is essentially unanimous on the fifth-rank smother. Colleagues 1,10,11,12,14,2,3,4,5,7,8,9 and now Colleague 13 all endorse the forced net 34… Rh5+ 35.Kf6 35… Ng4#. It is presented in the one-move shorthand as Rh5# / immediate mate on the 5th rank.

The flight-square enumeration repeated in round 3 is:
* Kxh5 illegal on …g6×h5, rook on h5 protected by g6 pawn;
* Kf5 met by …g6×f5 / Rh5×f5;
* Kg4 met by …Ne5×g4;
* Kh4/Kh6 met by Rh5×h4/Rh5×h6;
* Kf4 occupied by White Nf4;
* Kg6 occupied by …g6 pawn.
Hence the only nominal flight is Kf6, after which Ng4# is mate with the checking knight covered by the h5 rook / Re8 / g6 net and all adjacent squares e7/e6/f7/h6/h5/f5/g5/e5 sealed.

Colleague 13 has abandoned the earlier h6+ Kf6 Re6+ / h6+ Kf6 g5+ Kg4 Nh2# / h6+ Kf6 Nf7# proposals and now argues the h6+ line hands White an extra tempo and that Rh5+ exploits the g6 protection and Ne5 g4 coverage immediately. The earlier dissent about needing a preparatory pawn check is now treated as longer and unnecessary.

Unresolved formal tension carried from round 1/2: on the strict FEN derived from the move list, after 21.Bxe3 Qxe3 22.Rd3 Qxd3 23.Qxd3 Ne5 the queen pin on e3-f4-g5 is gone, so Nxh5 from White Nf4 to h5 is a legal capture on Rh5+. The public argument dismisses capture on h5 only via Kxh5 illegal on g6xh5 and assumes no interposition/capture on the rook. No colleague in round 3 addresses Nxh5. The Bc8×g4 cover invoked in earlier rounds is inconsistent with the actual bishop history Bb4-Bxc3-Ba6-Bxf1 captured on f1.

Current belief for group purposes remains the parsimonious fifth-rank smother promoted by the majority: the intended answer is Rh5+ Kf6 Ng4#, commonly shorthand Rh5#. The Nxh5 legality objection remains the remaining formal objection to an immediate mate claim; if Nxh5 were legal the line would be mate in two with Kf6 as the only non-capturing defence after the pin is removed.

